Recognition: the problem asks about a [[o:contiguous sequence]] (substring / subarray) that satisfies some property, and that property is [[g:monotonic]] — expanding the window can only make it "worse," while shrinking it can only make it "better." The pointers therefore never move backward → O(n).

**Variable-size (dynamic)** — the standard template:

<pre><code><span class="k">int</span> left = <span class="n">0</span>, best = <span class="n">0</span>;
<span class="k">for</span> (<span class="k">int</span> right = <span class="n">0</span>; right &lt; n; right++) {
    add(a[right]);                      <span class="c">// expand on the right</span>
    <span class="k">while</span> (!valid()) {                 <span class="c">// invalid =&gt; shrink from the left</span>
        remove(a[left]);
        left++;
    }
    best = Math.<span class="f">max</span>(best, right - left + <span class="n">1</span>);
}</code></pre>

**Fixed-size (constant k)** — no `while` is needed; just slide:

<pre><code><span class="k">for</span> (<span class="k">int</span> right = <span class="n">0</span>; right &lt; n; right++) {
    add(a[right]);
    <span class="k">if</span> (right &gt;= k) remove(a[right - k]);   <span class="c">// remove the element that fell out</span>
    <span class="k">if</span> (right &gt;= k - <span class="n">1</span>) record(left = right - k + <span class="n">1</span>);
}</code></pre>

Each element enters the window exactly once and leaves exactly once → [[g:O(n)]], even with a `while` nested inside the `for`. Interviewers often probe this point: *"Why is a while loop nested inside a for loop still O(n)?"*

:::deep
- The [[r:necessary]] condition for sliding window to work is that the property be monotonic with respect to window length. An array with [[r:negative numbers]] breaks that condition for "subarray sum ≥ k" → use prefix sums plus a deque or binary search, not sliding window.
- Shrinking with `while` versus `if`: use `while` for a "*longest* valid window" problem; use `if` for a fixed-size window.
- Window state is typically a frequency-counting `HashMap`, a running sum, or a monotonic `Deque` for window maximum/minimum problems.
- The "exactly k distinct characters" variant = `atMost(k) - atMost(k-1)` — a useful and frequently tested trick.
- Classic progression by difficulty: LC 3 (no repeated characters) → LC 76 (minimum window substring) → LC 30 (19-dsa-leetcode.the-solving-framework-core-patterns.q3) → LC 239 (sliding window maximum, which requires a deque).
:::

**Canonical problem:** count the subarrays whose sum is exactly `k`, where the array [[r:may contain negative numbers]].

Sliding window [[r:fails]] here: with negative numbers, expanding the window no longer increases the sum monotonically → there is no reliable signal for when to shrink it.

**Idea:** let `P[i]` be the prefix sum through `i`. The subarray `(j, i]` has sum `k` ⟺ `P[i] − P[j] = k` ⟺ `P[j] = P[i] − k`. We therefore only need to count how many times we have already seen the value `P[i] − k`.

<pre><code><span class="k">int</span> <span class="f">subarraySum</span>(<span class="k">int</span>[] a, <span class="k">int</span> k) {
    Map&lt;Integer,Integer&gt; seen = <span class="k">new</span> HashMap&lt;&gt;();
    seen.<span class="f">put</span>(<span class="n">0</span>, <span class="n">1</span>);              <span class="c">// empty prefix: important!</span>
    <span class="k">int</span> sum = <span class="n">0</span>, count = <span class="n">0</span>;
    <span class="k">for</span> (<span class="k">int</span> x : a) {
        sum += x;
        count += seen.<span class="f">getOrDefault</span>(sum - k, <span class="n">0</span>);
        seen.<span class="f">merge</span>(sum, <span class="n">1</span>, Integer::sum);
    }
    <span class="k">return</span> count;
}</code></pre>

O(n) time and O(n) space. The `seen.put(0, 1)` line handles the case where the prefix from the beginning already sums to `k` — [[r:forget it and the first test case fails immediately]].

:::deep
The same template produces several other solutions by changing what you store or count:

- **Sum divisible by k** → store `sum % k` (remember to normalize negative values: `((sum % k) + k) % k`)
- **Longest subarray with sum = k** → map each prefix sum to the [[o:first index]] where it appears, and insert only when it is absent
- **Equal numbers of 0s and 1s** → convert every 0 to −1, reducing the problem to sum = 0
- **Parity and XOR problems** → use prefix XOR with the analogous equation `P[i] ^ P[j] = k`

- In 2D, use two-dimensional prefix sums + inclusion–exclusion for O(1) rectangle-sum queries.
- If dynamic updates are required, replace the prefix array with a [[o:Fenwick tree / BIT]] so both queries and updates are O(log n).
- [[r:Integer overflow]]: summing 10⁵ elements as large as 10⁹ exceeds `int` → use `long`. Interviewers frequently probe this exact issue.
- State the boundary clearly: sliding window for [[g:non-negative arrays]], prefix sum + hash map for [[g:arrays that may contain negatives]]. Articulating this distinction is a strong signal.
:::

Both explore a state space; the difference is what the problem asks you to reuse or enumerate.

<table class="cmp"><thead><tr><th></th><th>Dynamic programming</th><th>Backtracking</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td>Signal</td><td>overlapping subproblems plus an optimal/count/feasible result</td><td>enumerate or find assignments under constraints</td></tr>
<tr><td>Core</td><td>cache one result per sufficient state</td><td>choose → recurse → undo, pruning invalid branches</td></tr>
<tr><td>Complexity</td><td>reachable states × transition cost</td><td>size of the explored search tree, often output-sensitive</td></tr>
</tbody></table>

For DP, write five things before code: state meaning, transition, base cases, evaluation order, and where the answer lives. Start top-down when it exposes the recurrence; convert to bottom-up when stack depth, iteration order, or memory locality matters.

Compress a dimension only after proving future transitions no longer need overwritten states. Store a predecessor/choice when the problem asks for the actual path, not just its score.

<pre><code><span class="c">// Top-down: state must contain every fact that affects future choices.</span>
long <span class="f">solve</span>(State s) {
    <span class="k">if</span> (s.<span class="f">done</span>()) <span class="k">return</span> s.<span class="f">baseValue</span>();
    <span class="k">if</span> (memo.<span class="f">containsKey</span>(s)) <span class="k">return</span> memo.<span class="f">get</span>(s);
    long best = IDENTITY;
    <span class="k">for</span> (Choice c : s.<span class="f">choices</span>())
        best = <span class="f">combine</span>(best, <span class="f">solve</span>(s.<span class="f">take</span>(c)));
    memo.<span class="f">put</span>(s, best);
    <span class="k">return</span> best;
}</code></pre>

For backtracking, define the partial assignment and invariant, generate only legal choices, apply one choice, recurse, then undo exactly that mutation. Sort when it enables duplicate skipping or monotone pruning.

If two paths reach the same sufficient state and only the best/count result matters, memoization often turns the search into DP.

:::deep
Common mistakes are memoizing an incomplete state, using a mutable collection as a map key, pruning without a proof, sharing a result list that later mutations corrupt, and claiming `O(2^n)` when pruning or output size dominates.

Validate the recurrence on a tiny brute-force oracle, include empty/minimal cases, and test reconstruction separately from the optimal value.
:::

Use a [[g:monotonic structure]] when an element that is worse than a newer element can never become useful again.

- A decreasing stack answers next/previous greater, stock span, and histogram-boundary questions. State whether it stores values or indices and whether equal values are popped; that equality choice defines boundaries and duplicate behavior.
- When an item is popped, the current index often finalizes its answer.
- A monotonic deque handles a moving window. Remove expired indices from the front, remove dominated candidates from the back, then add the new index. The front is the best live candidate. Every index enters and leaves at most once, giving amortized `O(n)`.

<pre><code>Deque&lt;Integer&gt; dq = <span class="k">new</span> ArrayDeque&lt;&gt;();
<span class="k">for</span> (int i = 0; i &lt; a.length; i++) {
    <span class="k">while</span> (!dq.<span class="f">isEmpty</span>() &amp;&amp; dq.<span class="f">peekFirst</span>() &lt;= i - k) dq.<span class="f">removeFirst</span>();
    <span class="k">while</span> (!dq.<span class="f">isEmpty</span>() &amp;&amp; a[dq.<span class="f">peekLast</span>()] &lt;= a[i]) dq.<span class="f">removeLast</span>();
    dq.<span class="f">addLast</span>(i);
}</code></pre>

For intervals, first define endpoint semantics. Half-open `[start,end)` intervals make adjacent ranges non-overlapping and match many time APIs. Sorting by start supports merge/insert/meeting-room scans.

A sweep line converts ranges into ordered start/end events or deltas; maintain the active count/state between events. For equal coordinates, event ordering must follow the endpoint definition—do not rely on an accidental comparator tie.

:::deep
Recognize the shared invariant: discard state only after proving it cannot affect a future answer. For stacks/deques that proof is dominance; for merged intervals it is coverage; for sweep lines it is that all events before the cursor are finalized.

Use `long` for coordinates/deltas when subtraction can overflow. With a huge sparse coordinate space, sort events or coordinate-compress; with a small bounded space, a difference array may be simpler. Test equal values, zero-length/adjacent intervals, duplicate endpoints, `k=1`, `k=n`, and empty input.
:::

Start with what must flow [[g:down]] from ancestors, what must flow [[g:up]] from children, and whether processing is preorder, inorder, postorder, or level order. DFS suits subtree aggregation and path state; BFS suits levels and unweighted distance.

Recursive DFS uses the call stack—on a degenerate tree, use an explicit stack or another strategy if depth can exceed safe limits.

For a BST, the invariant is global: every key in the left subtree belongs in the permitted lower range and every key in the right subtree in the upper range. Checking only `node.left &lt; node &lt; node.right` misses a violation deeper in a subtree.

Carry exclusive/inclusive bounds according to an explicit duplicate policy, and use a wide type or nullable bounds instead of computing `value ± 1` near numeric limits. Plain BST operations are `O(h)`, which becomes `O(n)` when unbalanced; `O(log n)` requires a balance guarantee.

<pre><code>boolean <span class="f">valid</span>(Node n, Long low, Long high) {
    <span class="k">if</span> (n == null) <span class="k">return</span> true;
    <span class="k">if</span> (low != null &amp;&amp; n.key &lt;= low) <span class="k">return</span> false;
    <span class="k">if</span> (high != null &amp;&amp; n.key &gt;= high) <span class="k">return</span> false;
    <span class="k">return</span> <span class="f">valid</span>(n.left, low, (long) n.key)
        &amp;&amp; <span class="f">valid</span>(n.right, (long) n.key, high);
}</code></pre>

A trie trades memory for prefix operations. Each edge represents a symbol; nodes need an end-of-key marker because a key can prefix another.

Choose child storage from the alphabet and workload: fixed arrays are fast but sparse, maps save sparse space, compressed/radix tries collapse one-child chains. Define normalization, case, and Unicode code-point/grapheme semantics before coding.

:::deep
Many “tree problems” are tree DP: return a small summary such as height, validity, best downward path, and best subtree answer from each child instead of rescanning subtrees. If the input can contain shared nodes or cycles, it is a graph, so ownership and a visited policy matter.

Test empty/single/skewed trees, duplicate policy, numeric boundaries, prefix-as-key, and non-ASCII input.
:::
